.PP
\fR#include <\fBdefine\&.h\fP>\fP
.PP
Enable FIPS 140-3 compliance mode\&. When defined, activates the full set of controls mandated by FIPS 140-3 (ISO/IEC 19790:2012) for a software cryptographic module\&. The following requirements are enforced:

.PP
[TODO]
.IP "\(bu" 2
Power-on self-tests (Section 9\&.5)\&. On every cold initialisation the module executes the Known-Answer Tests for each enabled primitive (AEAD128, Hash256, XOF128)\&. A failure places the module in a permanent error state from which no cryptographic output is produced and \fRVERUM_FAULT_HANDLER\fP is invoked\&. [TODO]
.IP "\(bu" 2
Conditional self-tests (Section 9\&.6)\&. A pair-wise consistency check is performed on each key prior to its first use in an encrypt or decrypt operation\&. If the check fails the key is rejected and \fRVERUM_FAULT_HANDLER\fP is invoked\&. [DONE]
.IP "\(bu" 2
Zeroization of sensitive data (Section 9\&.7)\&. All cryptographic variables — key copies, the Ascon permutation state, authentication tag intermediates, and XOF squeeze buffers — are overwritten with zeroes before the enclosing function returns\&. Stores are issued through \fRVERUM_ATTR_VOLATILE\fP pointers to prevent the optimiser from eliding them as dead stores\&.
.PP

.PP
Enabling this flag increases code size, startup latency, and per-call overhead\&. \fRVERUM_FAULT_HANDLER\fP must be defined when this flag is active\&. 
